### Why are trace IDs insufficient for long-running workflows?

Distributed traces are created at the edge and flow down synchronous calls. They are excellent for live latency debugging but have three properties that make them insufficient on their own.

```text
Property 1: Traces are short-lived and sampled.
  Most platforms retain traces for hours to 2 weeks.
  Sampling keeps only 10-20% of all traces.
  If a settlement completes on T+2 and is disputed a month later,
  the trace is long gone.

Property 2: Traces break at async boundaries.
  Service publishes to Kafka -> consumer starts a NEW trace.
  One business operation becomes many disconnected traces.
  Even with header propagation, days-long linkage is fragile.

Property 3: Trace IDs are not business-meaningful.
  You cannot query your database: "Find the trace for Rajesh's rent payment."
  The traceId was never stored as a column.
```

### What is a durable business identifier?

A durable business identifier is a stable, domain-meaningful key generated once when the operation is born. You persist it as an indexed column in every service's database, put it into every event, and include it in every log line.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class PaymentService {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(PaymentService.class.getName());

    public String initiateTransfer(String fromId, String toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Generate durable business ID once at birth
        String paymentId = "PAY-" + UUID.randomUUID().toString().substring(0, 8);

        // Persist as indexed column
        // paymentRepository.save(new Payment(paymentId, fromId, toId, amount));

        // Include in every log line alongside traceId
        LOG.info("paymentId=" + paymentId + " traceId=auto Transfer initiated "
            + fromId + " -> " + toId + " amount=" + amount);

        // Include in every event published to Kafka
        // kafkaTemplate.send("payment-events", paymentId, new TransferInitiated(...));

        return paymentId;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected log output:
paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=xyz789 Transfer initiated SAV2001 -> SAV2002 amount=50000.00
paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=xyz789 Debit completed for SAV2001
paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=abc123 Settlement submitted to correspondent bank
paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=def456 Settlement confirmed T+2
paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=ghi789 SMS sent to Rajesh

Note: traceId changes across days, but paymentId stays the same.
```

### How do you carry the business ID across services?

The business ID travels in the event payload, in HTTP headers, and in every log line. Unlike trace IDs, it is persisted in database columns.

```java
package com.bank.payment.event;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

// Every domain event carries the business ID
public record TransferInitiated(
    String paymentId,       // durable business ID
    String fromAccountId,
    String toAccountId,
    BigDecimal amount,
    Instant initiatedAt
) {}
```

```java
package com.bank.notification;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Component
public class NotificationConsumer {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(NotificationConsumer.class.getName());

    @KafkaListener(topics = "payment-events", groupId = "notification-group")
    public void onTransferEvent(TransferInitiated event) {
        // Business ID from event payload - persisted, never lost
        LOG.info("paymentId=" + event.paymentId() + " Sending SMS to customer");
        // notificationRepository.save(new NotificationRecord(event.paymentId(), ...));
    }
}
```

```text
Reconstruction query (months later):
SELECT * FROM payments WHERE payment_id = 'PAY-a1b2c3d4';
SELECT * FROM settlements WHERE payment_id = 'PAY-a1b2c3d4';
SELECT * FROM notifications WHERE payment_id = 'PAY-a1b2c3d4';

All traces expired, but the full story is reconstructable.
```

### How does the business ID serve as an idempotency key?

If Rajesh's app generates the paymentId (a UUID) and retries, the server recognizes the same ID and does not double-charge.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Service
public class IdempotentPaymentService {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(IdempotentPaymentService.class.getName());
    private final PaymentRepository paymentRepository;

    public IdempotentPaymentService(PaymentRepository paymentRepository) {
        this.paymentRepository = paymentRepository;
    }

    public void processTransfer(String paymentId, String fromId, String toId,
                                 BigDecimal amount) {
        if (paymentRepository.existsById(paymentId)) {
            LOG.info("paymentId=" + paymentId + " Duplicate request. Returning existing result.");
            return; // Idempotent: same paymentId -> same result, no double debit
        }
        // Process the transfer
        LOG.info("paymentId=" + paymentId + " Processing new transfer");
    }
}
```

```text
First request:  paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 -> processed, debit of 50000.00
Retry request:  paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 -> duplicate detected, no double debit

The same identifier serves three jobs:
1. Correlation (search logs)
2. Auditability (reconstruct from DB)
3. Idempotency (prevent duplicates)
```

### How do trace ID and business ID compare? (comparison table)

| Aspect | Trace ID | Business ID |
|---|---|---|
| Generated by | Tracing library (auto) | Application code (explicit) |
| Lifespan | Hours to weeks | Permanent (database column) |
| Scope | One synchronous request path | Entire business operation lifecycle |
| Breaks at Kafka | New trace starts | Same ID in event payload |
| Queryable in DB | No (only in tracing backend) | Yes (indexed column) |
| Sampled | Yes (10-20% typical) | Never (100% persisted) |
| Use | Live latency debugging | Audit trail, reconstruction, idempotency |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is relying only on trace IDs for operations that span days.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Only log traceId. Trace expires in 48 hours.
// When customer disputes a transfer a month later, nothing to search.
public class WrongLogging {
    // LOG.info("traceId=" + traceId + " Transfer initiated");
}

// CORRECT: Log both traceId AND paymentId.
// paymentId persisted in DB, searchable forever.
public class CorrectLogging {
    // LOG.info("paymentId=" + paymentId + " traceId=" + traceId + " Transfer initiated");
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The business ID should be client-supplied where possible (a UUID generated by the mobile app) so it doubles as an idempotency key. In banking, reconstructing an operation months later is a regulatory requirement. Propagate both trace ID and business ID everywhere: the trace for live debugging, the business ID for permanent audit.

### What about propagation in async and reactive contexts?

In synchronous code the business ID flows naturally through method parameters. In async code (Kafka consumers, scheduled jobs, reactive pipelines) you must explicitly extract it from the event payload or message header and place it into the MDC or context so every log line includes it. Failing to do this creates a blind spot where logs during async processing have no business ID.

```java
package com.bank.payment.async;

import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

@Component
public class AsyncContextPropagation {

    private static final Logger LOG = Logger.getLogger(AsyncContextPropagation.class.getName());

    @KafkaListener(topics = "payment-events", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onPaymentEvent(TransferInitiated event) {
        // CORRECT: extract business ID from event and set in MDC
        MDC.put("paymentId", event.paymentId());
        try {
            LOG.info("Processing settlement for " + event.paymentId());
            // All log lines in this thread now include paymentId
        } finally {
            MDC.clear(); // clean up after processing
        }
    }
}
```

```text
// WRONG: Kafka consumer logs without business ID
// traceId=abc123 Processing settlement  <-- which payment?

// CORRECT: extract paymentId from event into MDC
// paymentId=PAY-a1b2c3d4 traceId=abc123 Processing settlement
```
